## Tuesday — Pages and Routing

### File-Based Routing

In the Pages Router, **every file inside `pages/` automatically becomes a route** based on its file name and location. No route configuration files needed.

| File | Route |
|---|---|
| `pages/index.js` | `/` |
| `pages/about.js` | `/about` |
| `pages/contact.js` | `/contact` |
| `pages/blog/index.js` | `/blog` |
| `pages/blog/first-post.js` | `/blog/first-post` |

### Creating a New Page

```jsx
// pages/about.js
export default function About() {
  return <h1>About Us</h1>;
}
```

Visiting `/about` renders this component automatically.

### Navigation with `<Link>`

Use `next/link` instead of a plain `<a>` tag for **client-side navigation** (no full page reload):

```jsx
import Link from "next/link";

export default function Nav() {
  return (
    <nav>
      <Link href="/">Home</Link>
      <Link href="/about">About</Link>
      <Link href="/blog">Blog</Link>
    </nav>
  );
}
```

### Dynamic Routes

Square brackets `[param]` create dynamic segments:

```
pages/blog/[slug].js   → matches /blog/hello-world, /blog/anything
```

```jsx
// pages/blog/[slug].js
import { useRouter } from "next/router";

export default function BlogPost() {
  const router = useRouter();
  const { slug } = router.query;

  return <h1>Post: {slug}</h1>;
}
```

### Nested Dynamic Routes

```
pages/products/[category]/[id].js  → /products/shoes/42
```

```jsx
import { useRouter } from "next/router";

export default function Product() {
  const { category, id } = useRouter().query;
  return <p>Category: {category}, Product ID: {id}</p>;
}
```

### Catch-All Routes

```
pages/docs/[...slug].js   → matches /docs/a, /docs/a/b, /docs/a/b/c
```

```jsx
import { useRouter } from "next/router";

export default function Docs() {
  const { slug } = useRouter().query; // slug is an array
  return <p>Path: {Array.isArray(slug) ? slug.join("/") : slug}</p>;
}
```

Optional catch-all (also matches `/docs` itself): `pages/docs/[[...slug]].js`

### Programmatic Navigation

```jsx
import { useRouter } from "next/router";

export default function LoginButton() {
  const router = useRouter();

  const handleLogin = () => {
    // ...auth logic
    router.push("/dashboard");
  };

  return <button onClick={handleLogin}>Log In</button>;
}
```

### Custom 404 Page

```jsx
// pages/404.js
export default function Custom404() {
  return <h1>404 — Page Not Found</h1>;
}
```

### Takeaways

- File name/path in `pages/` = the route.
- `[param]` = dynamic segment, `[...param]` = catch-all.
- `<Link>` for declarative navigation, `useRouter()` for reading params and programmatic navigation.

