## Wednesday — Static Site Generation (SSG)

### What is SSG?

With **Static Site Generation**, HTML pages are generated **once, at build time**, and then reused for every request. This makes pages extremely fast (served from a CDN, no server work per request) and great for SEO.

Best for: blogs, marketing pages, documentation, product listings that don't change per-request.

### `getStaticProps`

Export an async function called `getStaticProps` from a page to fetch data at **build time**:

```jsx
// pages/blog/index.js
export default function Blog({ posts }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

export async function getStaticProps() {
  const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/posts");
  const posts = await res.json();

  return {
    props: { posts }, // passed to the page component as props
  };
}
```

Key points:

- Runs **only on the server**, at build time — never shipped to the browser.
- Can read files, query a database, or call an API directly.
- Must return an object with a `props` key.

### `getStaticPaths` (for Dynamic Routes)

When a dynamic route (`[slug].js`) uses `getStaticProps`, Next.js needs to know **which paths to pre-render**. That's what `getStaticPaths` is for:

```jsx
// pages/blog/[slug].js
export default function Post({ post }) {
  return (
    <article>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p>{post.body}</p>
    </article>
  );
}

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/posts");
  const posts = await res.json();

  const paths = posts.map((post) => ({
    params: { slug: post.slug },
  }));

  return {
    paths,
    fallback: false, // any other route returns 404
  };
}

export async function getStaticProps({ params }) {
  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/posts/${params.slug}`);
  const post = await res.json();

  return { props: { post } };
}
```

### `fallback` Options

| Value | Behavior |
|---|---|
| `false` | Paths not returned by `getStaticPaths` show a 404. |
| `true` | Unlisted paths render a fallback loading state, then generate on first request. |
| `"blocking"` | Unlisted paths are generated on the server on first request (no loading state, page waits). |

### Incremental Static Regeneration (ISR)

ISR lets you update static pages **after** build, without a full rebuild, using `revalidate`:

```jsx
export async function getStaticProps() {
  const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/posts");
  const posts = await res.json();

  return {
    props: { posts },
    revalidate: 60, // regenerate this page at most once every 60 seconds
  };
}
```

How it works: the first request after 60 seconds still gets the (possibly stale) cached page instantly, but Next.js regenerates the page in the background and swaps it in for subsequent requests.

### When to Use SSG

✅ Content doesn't change per-user (blogs, docs, marketing pages)
✅ SEO and load speed are priorities
✅ Data can be a little stale (use ISR if it changes occasionally)

❌ Avoid for highly personalized or real-time data (use SSR or CSR instead)

### Takeaways

- `getStaticProps` → fetch data at build time.
- `getStaticPaths` → required for dynamic SSG routes; defines which pages to pre-build.
- `revalidate` → enables ISR, refreshing static pages periodically without a full rebuild.

