## Thursday — Server-Side Rendering (SSR)

### What is SSR?

With **Server-Side Rendering**, the HTML for a page is generated **on every request**, on the server, using fresh data — then sent to the browser already rendered.

Best for: dashboards, pages with per-user/session data, frequently changing content, content that must always be up to date.

### `getServerSideProps`

```jsx
// pages/dashboard.js
export default function Dashboard({ user, stats }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Welcome back, {user.name}</h1>
      <p>Total sales today: {stats.salesToday}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export async function getServerSideProps(context) {
  const { req, res, params, query } = context;

  const statsRes = await fetch("https://api.example.com/stats");
  const stats = await statsRes.json();

  const user = { name: "Asha" }; // e.g., derived from a session/cookie

  return {
    props: { user, stats },
  };
}
```

Key points:

- Runs on **every request**, server-side only.
- Has access to `req`, `res`, `params`, and `query` via the `context` object — useful for reading cookies, headers, or redirecting.
- Slower time-to-first-byte than SSG (since data is fetched per request), but always fresh.

### Redirecting from `getServerSideProps`

```jsx
export async function getServerSideProps(context) {
  const isLoggedIn = false; // check session/cookie here

  if (!isLoggedIn) {
    return {
      redirect: {
        destination: "/login",
        permanent: false,
      },
    };
  }

  return { props: {} };
}
```

### SSG vs SSR vs CSR — Comparison

| | SSG | SSR | CSR |
|---|---|---|---|
| **When HTML is generated** | Build time | Every request | In the browser, after JS loads |
| **Speed (TTFB)** | Fastest | Slower (server work per request) | Fast shell, slow content |
| **Data freshness** | Can go stale (unless ISR) | Always fresh | Always fresh |
| **SEO** | Excellent | Excellent | Weaker (needs JS execution) |
| **Server load** | Low (served from CDN) | Higher (renders per request) | Low (client does the work) |
| **Good for** | Blogs, docs, marketing | Dashboards, personalized pages | Highly interactive apps, admin panels |
| **Next.js function** | `getStaticProps` | `getServerSideProps` | `useEffect` + `fetch`, or client hooks |

### When to Use SSR

✅ Data changes on every request (stock prices, live scores)
✅ Content is personalized per logged-in user
✅ You need request-time info (cookies, headers, geolocation)

❌ Avoid if the same content could be pre-built once (use SSG/ISR instead — it's faster and cheaper)

### Takeaways

- `getServerSideProps` → fetch fresh data on every request, server-side.
- Use the `context` object to access `req`, `res`, `params`, `query`, and to redirect.
- SSR trades some speed for guaranteed freshness — choose it deliberately, not by default.

