## Friday — Mini Project: Static Site

### Goal

Build a small **statically generated blog** using `getStaticProps` and `getStaticPaths`, reinforcing this week's concepts end-to-end.

### Folder Structure

```
my-blog/
├── posts/
│   ├── hello-world.md
│   ├── learning-nextjs.md
│   └── static-vs-server.md
├── pages/
│   ├── index.js
│   ├── posts/
│   │   └── [slug].js
│   └── _app.js
├── lib/
│   └── posts.js
├── styles/
│   └── globals.css
└── package.json
```

### 1. Install Markdown Dependencies

```bash
npm install gray-matter remark remark-html
```

- `gray-matter` — parses front-matter metadata from markdown files
- `remark` / `remark-html` — converts markdown content to HTML

### 2. Add Sample Markdown Posts

```md
---
title: "Hello World"
date: "2026-08-10"
---
This is my **first post** using Next.js Static Site Generation!
```

Save similar files as `posts/learning-nextjs.md` and `posts/static-vs-server.md`.

### 3. Helper Functions (`lib/posts.js`)

```js
import fs from "fs";
import path from "path";
import matter from "gray-matter";
import { remark } from "remark";
import html from "remark-html";

const postsDirectory = path.join(process.cwd(), "posts");

export function getAllPostSlugs() {
  const fileNames = fs.readdirSync(postsDirectory);
  return fileNames.map((fileName) => ({
    params: { slug: fileName.replace(/\.md$/, "") },
  }));
}

export function getSortedPostsData() {
  const fileNames = fs.readdirSync(postsDirectory);
  const allPostsData = fileNames.map((fileName) => {
    const slug = fileName.replace(/\.md$/, "");
    const fullPath = path.join(postsDirectory, fileName);
    const fileContents = fs.readFileSync(fullPath, "utf8");
    const { data } = matter(fileContents);

    return { slug, ...data };
  });

  return allPostsData.sort((a, b) => (a.date < b.date ? 1 : -1));
}

export async function getPostData(slug) {
  const fullPath = path.join(postsDirectory, `${slug}.md`);
  const fileContents = fs.readFileSync(fullPath, "utf8");
  const { data, content } = matter(fileContents);

  const processedContent = await remark().use(html).process(content);
  const contentHtml = processedContent.toString();

  return { slug, contentHtml, ...data };
}
```

### 4. Home Page — List All Posts (`pages/index.js`)

```jsx
import Link from "next/link";
import { getSortedPostsData } from "../lib/posts";

export default function Home({ allPostsData }) {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>My Static Blog</h1>
      <ul>
        {allPostsData.map(({ slug, title, date }) => (
          <li key={slug}>
            <Link href={`/posts/${slug}`}>{title}</Link>
            <br />
            <small>{date}</small>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </main>
  );
}

export async function getStaticProps() {
  const allPostsData = getSortedPostsData();
  return {
    props: { allPostsData },
  };
}
```

### 5. Individual Post Page (`pages/posts/[slug].js`)

```jsx
import { getAllPostSlugs, getPostData } from "../../lib/posts";

export default function Post({ postData }) {
  return (
    <article>
      <h1>{postData.title}</h1>
      <p><small>{postData.date}</small></p>
      <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: postData.contentHtml }} />
    </article>
  );
}

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const paths = getAllPostSlugs();
  return {
    paths,
    fallback: false,
  };
}

export async function getStaticProps({ params }) {
  const postData = await getPostData(params.slug);
  return {
    props: { postData },
  };
}
```

### 6. Run It

```bash
npm run dev
```

Visit `http://localhost:3000` — you should see a list of blog posts, each linking to its own statically generated page.

### 7. Build for Production

```bash
npm run build
npm run start
```

During `npm run build`, watch the terminal output — Next.js will list each route and mark static ones with a `●` (SSG) symbol, confirming the pages were pre-rendered at build time.

### 8. Deploy (Optional)

The fastest path is Vercel (made by the Next.js team):

```bash
npm install -g vercel
vercel
```

Follow the CLI prompts to deploy; every push can be configured to auto-deploy.

